# Analytics Dashboard Implementation Walkthrough

The Analytics Dashboard has been successfully built and deployed! You now have a professional, Grafana-style metrics hub right inside your application.

## What Was Built

### 1. The Alarms Archive Ledger
I implemented the `alarms_archive.json` background ledger.
- Whenever an alarm is created, it goes to both the active queue and the historical archive.
- When you **Dismiss (False Positive)** an alarm, it disappears from your dashboard but is marked as `DISMISSED` in the archive.
- When you **Fix & Replay Sandbox** to generate a new rule, it is marked as `RESOLVED` in the archive.
- This ensures you *never* lose historical data for analytics, even when testing and clearing the active queue.

### 2. High-Performance APIs
I created a robust `/analytics` endpoint that correlates data between the `governance_audit.json` log (to measure total system traffic) and the new `alarms_archive.json` ledger (to measure threats). The API dynamically calculates Time-Series trends, Categorical breakdowns, and Resolution ratios.

### 3. Analytics Dashboard UI
I built a completely new tab in your application powered by **Recharts**:
- **Timeframe Control & Refresh**: A highly visible dropdown to select your window (Last 24 Hours, 7 Days, 30 Days, 3 Months, All Time) and a **Run Query / Refresh** button to fetch data manually, ensuring it handles cleared test queues gracefully.
- **KPI Cards**: Giant metrics tracking *Total Processed*, *Alarms Triggered*, *Alarms Resolved*, and a *System Health Score*.
- **Category Breakdown**: A horizontal Bar Chart showing exactly which data types are leaking the most (Authentication, GDPR, Financial).
- **Resolution Status**: A Donut Pie Chart visualizing the percentage of Pending vs Resolved vs Dismissed alarms.
- **Traffic vs Threats Trend**: A large Line Chart comparing your total processed traffic against the number of alarms triggered over time.

## Verification
You can verify the functionality by clicking on the new **Analytics & Metrics** tab in the main header. Select your timeframe and click the refresh button to see the graphs render based on your system logs.

Enjoy your new metrics!
